<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Like Sancocho! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Time to Eat.</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like Sancocho!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose short texts using simple phrases and sentences. Interactive: Respond to written questions from peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose short texts for images of food with simple words (e.g., "This is my lunch."). Can respond to written questions from peers about food choices (e.g., "Do you like bananas?" "Yes, I do."). Can write short notes for a lunch order (e.g., "I want a hamburger.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards of various foods (e.g., sancocho, rice with chicken, apple, banana, water, juice, sandwich). Students chant the names of the foods. The teacher then quickly writes on the board "I like ____." and "I want ____." and asks students to complete orally for a few examples. This activates previously learned vocabulary and sentence structures for writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple sentence on the board: "I like sancocho." The teacher then points to an image of an apple and models writing: "I like apples." The teacher introduces the compound sentence: "I like apples, but I don't like bananas." The teacher also models writing a simple "yes/no" question and answer: "Do you like soup?" and then writes "Yes, I do." or "No, I don't." on the board, emphasizing proper capitalization and punctuation. The teacher draws attention to the written form of /f/ in "favorite" and /s/ in "snack" during the writing process.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I write about my favorite food or my favorite color?" (My favorite food)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'I like sancocho, but I don't like bananas,' what does 'but' tell us?" (Two different things, one I like and one I don't like.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's Time to Eat."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a large poster or projected image of a simple written "Lunch Order Form" from a school cafeteria or a friend's lunch box note. The text includes simple sentences like:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Good morning, Ana!</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This is my lunch. I want rice with chicken and juice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I like sancocho, but I don't like apples.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What's your favorite food?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Your friend, Pedro."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Asking for and giving information about preferences: Expressing likes and dislikes about food, and asking about others' food preferences. Sociolinguistic: Sharing preferences: Respectfully expressing likes and dislikes about food.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the "Lunch Order Form" silently. Then, the teacher asks written gist questions on the board for students to answer in their notebooks or on mini-whiteboards:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who wrote the note?" (Pedro)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does Pedro want for lunch?" (Rice with chicken and juice)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does Pedro like apples? Write 'Yes, he does' or 'No, he doesn't'." (No, he doesn't)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: WH- and yes/no questions, Present simple, Compound sentences with "but". Vocabulary: apple, banana, sandwich, lunch, breakfast, dinner, food, snack, water, juice, rice with chicken, soup, eat, drink, taste, choose, bring, want, enjoy, share, prepare, serve, can, can't, tasty, healthy, hungry, favorite, sweet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with images of food and incomplete sentences. Students complete the sentences with appropriate vocabulary (e.g., "This is a ____." for a sandwich image; "I want ____." for a juice image). Students also complete sentences using "like" and "don't like": "I ____ sancocho." "I ____ eat bananas."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Builder with 'but'):</b> The teacher provides a list of foods (e.g., rice with chicken, soup, apple, banana) and two columns: "I like..." and "I don't like...". Students choose one item from each column and write sentences using "but". Example: "I like rice with chicken, but I don't like soup." This practices compound sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Written Q&A):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student writes three simple "yes/no" questions about food preferences on a piece of paper (e.g., "Do you like apples?", "Do you want water?"). They exchange papers with their partner and write short answers to their partner's questions (e.g., "Yes, I do." or "No, I don't."). The teacher circulates, monitoring for correct spelling, capitalization, and simple sentence structure.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's Time to Eat."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual Written Product – "My Lunch Order and Preferences" Note.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students imagine they are writing a note to their parent or a cafeteria worker to order their lunch and state their preferences. The teacher provides a template or a blank paper. Students must include:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A greeting (e.g., "Dear Mom," or "Hello Cafeteria,")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What they want for lunch (e.g., "I want rice with chicken and water.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One food they like (e.g., "I like sancocho.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One food they don't like, using "but" (e.g., "but I don't like bananas.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A closing (e.g., "Thank you," "Your child," and their name).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher encourages students to use the target vocabulary (e.g., tasty, healthy, favorite) and grammar structures (present simple, 'but' sentences) to express themselves. The focus is on producing a coherent short written text that fulfills the communicative purpose.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation and a simple checklist for the written notes. The teacher collects the "My Lunch Order and Preferences" notes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' written "Lunch Order and Preferences" notes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used simple sentences to state wants/preferences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Included target vocabulary (food items, like/don't like).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used "but" for compound sentences (e.g., "I like... but I don't...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legible handwriting for 3rd Grade.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responded to prompts/included required information.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can write what food I want for lunch. (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can write one food I like and one food I don't like. (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection for continuous improvement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy to write about food today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was difficult or what do I need more practice with in writing about food?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., Students effectively used "but" in compound sentences, or most students could write their lunch order clearly).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to support students' writing skills? (e.g., More focus on spelling common food words, more sentence starters for expressing likes/dislikes, review of WH-questions for written responses).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The writing skills developed today, particularly composing short texts and expressing preferences, will be essential for the Lesson 5 project, which will likely involve creating a food-related product (e.g., a simple menu, a recipe card, or a poster with food descriptions).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>